    "updatedInput": {
      "command": "L=~/.claude/skills/gstack/bin/gstack-learnings-log\n$L '{\"skill\":\"plan-eng-review\",\"type\":\"pitfall\",\"key\":\"git-push-track-empty-without-upstream\",\"insight\":\"git %(push:short)/%(push:track) in for-each-ref resolve to NOTHING for a branch with no upstream under push.default=simple (gits BUILT-IN default) and under upstream/nothing; only current and matching populate them. Verified on git 2.55. Every Workroom workroom is created by `git worktree add -b` so it has no upstream, meaning any ahead/behind derived from %(push:*) is permanently blank on a stock machine — and blank under GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null, so an integration test written to pin it also fails. This machine has push.default=current, which is the ONLY reason it looked correct in measurement. Use an explicit `git rev-list --left-right --count HEAD...refs/remotes/<remote>/<branch>` instead; it is config-independent and also dissolves the case where %(upstream) points at a LOCAL branch. Lesson: any measurement of git config-dependent behaviour must be repeated with configs isolated before it is trusted.\",\"confidence\":9,\"source\":\"cross-model\",\"files\":[\"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/VCSProviding.swift\",\"internal/vcs/git.go\"]}'\n$L '{\"skill\":\"plan-eng-review\",\"type\":\"pitfall\",\"key\":\"fetch-head-is-per-worktree\",\"insight\":\"FETCH_HEAD is PER-WORKTREE, not shared. `git rev-parse --git-path FETCH_HEAD` returns .git/FETCH_HEAD at a project root but .git/worktrees/<name>/FETCH_HEAD inside a workroom, and a fetch at the root does NOT create the workrooms copy (verified git 2.55). So fetching at the project root — which is right, because it gives every workroom one shared answer — must be paired with reading the COMMON git dir: parse the `gitdir:` line out of .git-as-a-file and strip the trailing /worktrees/<name>. Reading the per-worktree path instead makes every workroom report never-fetched forever. The same trap applies to every other per-worktree git file: HEAD, index, ORIG_HEAD, rebase-merge.\",\"confidence\":9,\"source\":\"cross-model\",\"files\":[\"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/VCSProviding.swift\"]}'\n$L '{\"skill\":\"plan-eng-review\",\"type\":\"operational\",\"key\":\"statuscommandrunner-env-and-stdin-gaps\",\"insight\":\"macapp StatusCommandRunner (:45-63) launches via /usr/bin/env, sets ONLY PATH plus GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS and GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0, unsets GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF, and NEVER assigns proc.standardInput — so the child inherits the apps stdin (a live terminal under `make app-run`) and cannot see anything from the users shell. Harmless while it only ran git status and gh, but any command that can PROMPT breaks: GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 does not govern ssh, so an ssh passphrase or unknown host key can stall to the full timeout; and SSH_AUTH_SOCK (1Password/gpg-agent), GIT_SSH_COMMAND, GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL and XDG_CONFIG_HOME are invisible, so agent users cannot authenticate at all. Fix: standardInput = FileHandle.nullDevice, GIT_SSH_COMMAND with BatchMode=yes, SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=never, and forward the auth vars from the EXISTING ShellEnvironment.environment() probe (:292) which is currently reserved for setup/teardown scripts. Entitlements are NOT a factor — project.yml:267,296-299 is hardened runtime with no App Sandbox and no entitlements file.\",\"confidence\":9,\"source\":\"cross-model\",\"files\":[\"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/StatusCommandRunner.swift\",\"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/ShellEnvironment.swift\"]}'\n$L '{\"skill\":\"plan-eng-review\",\"type\":\"pitfall\",\"key\":\"jj-new-on-current-bookmark-strands-work\",\"insight\":\"In a jj WORKSPACE (a Workroom workroom) `@` carries no bookmark, so current_ref reports the nearest ANCESTOR bookmark — the UI therefore shows e.g. main for every workspace of the project, and that value is SHARED, not per-workroom. Running `jj new <that same bookmark>`, the obvious implementation of switch-to-branch, was reproduced REMOVING a file from the working copy and leaving the workrooms commit as a nameless dangling head reachable only by change id, with no visible change in the UI. Any such affordance needs a target==current guard; more fundamentally, for jj current-branch is not a workroom property so a git-shaped branch switcher does not map. Related: all workrooms of a jj project share one repo and op-store, so `jj rebase` marks SIBLING workspaces stale and the resulting error surfaces in a different workroom than the one acted in.\",\"confidence\":8,\"source\":\"cross-model\",\"files\":[\"macapp/WorkroomApp/Core/RustJJProvider.swift\",\"internal/vcs/jj.go\"]}'\n$L '{\"skill\":\"plan-eng-review\",\"type\":\"operational\",\"key\":\"codex-outside-voice-blocked-in-sandbox\",\"insight\":\"In this sandbox `codex exec` fails at startup with \\\"failed to initialize in-process app-server client: Operation not permitted (os error 1)\\\" and must be re-run with dangerouslyDisableSandbox; even unsandboxed it exceeded the skills 5-minute ceiling on a 43KB plan, so budget for the Claude-subagent fallback rather than expecting Codex. Related sandbox facts: mktemp under /tmp is denied so use $TMPDIR, gstack-learnings-log itself needs the system temp dir and so must run unsandboxed, and dcg pattern-matches the COMMAND TEXT so even quoting a dangerous command inside a JSON payload is blocked.\",\"confidence\":8,\"source\":\"observed\",\"files\":[]}'\necho \"=== verify:\"; rtk grep -c \"push-track-empty\\|fetch-head-is-per-worktree\\|statuscommandrunner-env\\|jj-new-on-current\\|codex-outside-voice\" ~/.gstack/projects/joelmoss-workroom/learnings.jsonl",
